{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_vrtx_01UUAxP6BoTakgL37eX82TG4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. Here's what was written for **Q6: System processing huge PDF reports — how handle context when splitting documents**:\n\n**Key takeaways in the answer:**\n\n1. **Structure-aware extraction first** — PyMuPDF for native-text PDFs, AWS Textract for scanned/complex layouts. Detect section hierarchy via font-size heuristics before chunking.\n\n2. **Global metadata injection** — The \"amounts in thousands\" problem is the central gotcha. Extract document-wide context (currency units, date ranges, jurisdiction) during ingestion, store as metadata on every chunk, and prepend a one-line **document preamble** to the system prompt at generation time. Without this, the model misreads `$4,200` as $4,200 instead of $4.2M.\n\n3. **Parent-child (small-to-big) chunking** — 256-token child chunks for precise retrieval; 1,024-token parent chunks (section-aligned) returned to the LLM for full context. This is LlamaIndex's `SentenceWindowNodeParser` pattern.\n\n4. **Table handling** — Extract tables as Markdown, always keep the caption with the table as a single chunk. Never split mid-row.\n\n5. **Overlap windows** — 10–20% token overlap between adjacent chunks prevents sentences from being cut at boundaries.\n\n**Three pitfalls called out:** fixed-size character chunking (shreds tables and cross-section context), ignoring global metadata (the model can't infer \"thousands\" from a 256-token chunk), and treating all PDFs the same (scanned docs need OCR routing with confidence-score gating)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":299,"cache_read_input_tokens":58543,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":299,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0bc54a8